                "remediation": "Follow checks-effects-interactions: validate inputs, update all relevant contract state, and only then invoke another contract. If an external call must happen first, add an explicit reentrancy guard and document why the intermediate state is safe to observe.",
                "severity": "high"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Cross-contract call before state update"
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A public contract entry point passes a caller-supplied amount to `transfer` or `transfer_from` without comparing it against zero. Token implementations differ in how they treat negative or out-of-range amounts; a missing guard can therefore move an unintended value, corrupt accounting, or revert in surprising ways. This is a heuristic: it cannot prove that the token rejects the value or that another layer validates it, so it reports low confidence."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "## Token transfer amount without validation\n\nA public contract entry point passes a caller-supplied amount to `transfer` or `transfer_from` without comparing it against zero. Token implementations differ in how they treat negative or out-of-range amounts; a missing guard can therefore move an unintended value, corrupt accounting, or revert in surprising ways. This is a heuristic: it cannot prove that the token rejects the value or that another layer validates it, so it reports low confidence.\n\n### Remediation\n\nValidate the amount before transferring, for example reject `amount <= 0` with a typed contract error (or check both bounds when the token has a maximum). Validate once at the entry point and pass the checked value to `transfer`/`transfer_from`.\n\n### References\n\n- https://developers.stellar.org/docs/tokens/token-interface\n- https://developers.stellar.org/docs/learn/encyclopedia/security",
                "text": "A public contract entry point passes a caller-supplied amount to `transfer` or `transfer_from` without comparing it against zero. Token implementations differ in how they treat negative or out-of-range amounts; a missing guard can therefore move an unintended value, corrupt accounting, or revert in surprising ways. This is a heuristic: it cannot prove that the token rejects the value or that another layer validates it, so it reports low confidence.\n\nRemediation: Validate the amount before transferring, for example reject `amount <= 0` with a typed contract error (or check both bounds when the token has a maximum). Validate once at the entry point and pass the checked value to `transfer`/`transfer_from`."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://developers.stellar.org/docs/tokens/token-interface",
              "id": "SS-014",
              "name": "Token transfer amount without validation",
              "properties": {
                "category": "token",
