              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Instance storage is loaded for every invocation and has strict size limits. Writing repeated distinct keys, or growing a collection, in instance storage increases the cost of every call and can break the contract at scale. Constant configuration is a better fit for instance storage."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "## Unbounded data stored in instance storage\n\nInstance storage is loaded for every invocation and has strict size limits. Writing repeated distinct keys, or growing a collection, in instance storage increases the cost of every call and can break the contract at scale. Constant configuration is a better fit for instance storage.\n\n### Remediation\n\nKeep instance storage for small, fixed configuration. Store growing collections in persistent storage, and bound their size explicitly.\n\n### References\n\n- https://developers.stellar.org/docs/learn/encyclopedia/storage/persisting-data",
                "text": "Instance storage is loaded for every invocation and has strict size limits. Writing repeated distinct keys, or growing a collection, in instance storage increases the cost of every call and can break the contract at scale. Constant configuration is a better fit for instance storage.\n\nRemediation: Keep instance storage for small, fixed configuration. Store growing collections in persistent storage, and bound their size explicitly."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://developers.stellar.org/docs/learn/encyclopedia/storage/persisting-data",
